namespace Death {
//###==##====#=====--==~--~=~- --- -- -  -  -   -

/** @page multiplayer Multiplayer
@brief Guide how to run, configure and join multiplayer servers

@tableofcontents

@m_class{m-noindent}

Multiplayer comes in two flavors, both optional at build time:

-   <b>Local splitscreen</b> @m_span{m-text m-dim} (`WITH_MULTIPLAYER`) @m_endspan --- two to four players
    on one machine, no networking. Every game mode can be played this way.
-   <b>Online multiplayer</b> @m_span{m-text m-dim} (`WITH_ONLINE_MULTIPLAYER`, plus `WITH_WEBSOCKET` for the
    WebSocket transport) @m_endspan --- players connect to a server, either the game itself or a headless
    dedicated server.

@m_class{m-noindent}

Online sessions are <b>server-authoritative</b>: the server runs the whole game (damage, collectibles,
scores, rounds) and clients send input, so the level has to be present on the server. The same executable is
both game and server unless built with `DEDICATED_SERVER`. Multiplayer requires threads
@m_span{m-text m-dim} (`NCINE_WITH_THREADS`) @m_endspan, see @ref building-config-params-game.

@section multiplayer-hosting Running a server

@subsection multiplayer-hosting-listen Hosting from the game

In the main menu choose <b>Play Multiplayer</b> and then:

-   <b>Create Public Server</b> --- announced, see @ref multiplayer-visibility
-   <b>Create Private Server</b> --- hidden, joinable only by address
-   <b>Create Local Splitscreen Game</b> --- no networking, extra players use gamepads or a shared keyboard

@m_class{m-noindent}

Then pick the level (or the configured playlist) and the game mode. The host plays as a normal player but
must be reachable (see @ref multiplayer-visibility for the port to forward). All other settings come from
@cpp "Jazz2.Server.config" @ce (see @ref multiplayer-config), the same file a dedicated server reads.

@subsection multiplayer-hosting-dedicated Running a dedicated server

Start the executable with `/server`, optionally followed by the configuration path:

@code{.sh}
./jazz2 /server Jazz2.Server.config
@endcode

-   No window, graphics or audio; the simulation runs at a fixed 60 FPS and console commands are read from
    standard input, so it works over SSH, under a service manager or in a container.
-   `/server` works on <b>Linux</b>, <b>macOS</b> and, in debug builds only, <b>Windows</b>. A release
    Windows server is the `DEDICATED_SERVER` build: it is always a server and takes the configuration path
    as its <b>first argument</b>, without `/server`. Client arguments are not available in that build. See
    @ref building-config-params-game.
-   It needs the same game files as a normal installation: the original <b>Jazz Jackrabbit 2</b> data in
    @cpp "Source" @ce, converted into @cpp "Cache" @ce on first start.
-   It needs a <b>playlist</b> --- the starting level comes from the current playlist entry; without one it
    refuses to start with <em>"Server cannot be started because of invalid configuration"</em>.
-   A relative configuration path is resolved against the directory of the preferences file
    (@cpp "Jazz2.config" @ce, movable with `/config`); an absolute path is used as is. Without the argument,
    @cpp "Jazz2.Server.config" @ce is loaded from that directory.

@m_class{m-noindent}

A line on standard input starting with `/` is a command (see @ref multiplayer-commands), anything else is
broadcast as chat from the server. `/exit`, `/quit`, `Ctrl+C`, `SIGINT`/`SIGTERM` and the Windows console
close events shut it down cleanly (peers disconnected, server delisted); a second signal during shutdown
kills the process.

@m_class{m-noindent}

Tip: a server whose playlist has a single entry with @cpp "GameMode": "race" @ce traces the race
<b>minimap track</b> of an original level into the log right after loading (see
@ref Jazz2::Multiplayer::GenerateRaceRouteFromGeometry), without needing a client or a running round.

@subsection multiplayer-hosting-container Running a dedicated server as a container

A multi-architecture container image with the `DEDICATED_SERVER` configuration is published as well. See
@ref building-docker for the image, its volumes and the `docker-compose.yml`, including running several
instances on one host.

@section multiplayer-config Configuring a server

@subsection multiplayer-config-file Configuration file

The server is configured by one JSON file, by default @cpp "Jazz2.Server.config" @ce next to the
preferences file. Block and single-line comments are allowed, and @cpp "$include" @ce merges in another
file (recursively, each file only once), so several instances can share common settings. A minimal server
rotating two levels:

@code{.json}
{
	"ServerName": "{PlayerName}'s Server",
	"ServerPort": 7438,
	"MaxPlayerCount": 16,
	"MinPlayerCount": 2,

	"Playlist": [
		{ "LevelName": "battle1", "GameMode": "battle", "TotalKills": 15 },
		{ "LevelName": "arace1", "GameMode": "race", "TotalLaps": 3 }
	],
	"PlaylistIndex": 0
}
@endcode

@m_class{m-noindent}

@ref Jazz2::Multiplayer::ServerConfiguration documents <b>every</b> property, its default and a fully
annotated example. Overview:

Group | Properties
----- | ----------
Identity and reachability | @cpp "ServerName" @ce, @cpp "ServerPort" @ce, @cpp "ServerAddressOverride" @ce, @cpp "IsPrivate" @ce, WebSocket: @cpp "WsPort" @ce, @cpp "WsCertPath" @ce, @cpp "WsKeyPath" @ce
Access control | @cpp "ServerPassword" @ce, @cpp "MaxPlayerCount" @ce, @cpp "AllowedPlayerTypes" @ce, @cpp "RequiresDiscordAuth" @ce, player ID lists (see @ref multiplayer-config-access)
Round rules | @cpp "GameMode" @ce, @cpp "InitialPlayerHealth" @ce, @cpp "TotalKills" @ce, @cpp "TotalLaps" @ce, @cpp "TotalTreasureCollected" @ce, @cpp "MaxGameTimeSecs" @ce, @cpp "Elimination" @ce, team properties, gameplay switches such as @cpp "ReforgedGameplay" @ce, @cpp "AllowLedgeClimb" @ce, @cpp "PlayerStacking" @ce
Session flow | @cpp "MinPlayerCount" @ce and @cpp "PreGameSecs" @ce (when a round starts), @cpp "TotalPlayerPoints" @ce (championship length), @cpp "AllowJoinDuringRound" @ce, @cpp "JoinCooldownSecs" @ce, @cpp "ReconnectWindowSecs" @ce, @cpp "IdleKickTimeSecs" @ce
Operations | @cpp "AllowAssetStreaming" @ce (clients download missing levels from the server), @cpp "WebhookUrl" @ce and @cpp "WebhookEvents" @ce (post selected events to a <b>Discord</b> channel)

@m_class{m-noindent}

@cpp "ServerName" @ce and @cpp "WelcomeMessage" @ce accept @cpp "{PlayerName}" @ce and
@cpp "{ServerName}" @ce, and support @ref Jazz2-UI-Font-format "text formatting".

@subsection multiplayer-config-playlist Playlist

@cpp "Playlist" @ce is an array of rounds, each with a level name and usually a game mode. Properties an
entry doesn't set are inherited from the root, so the root holds common rules and entries only override
(see @ref Jazz2::Multiplayer::PlaylistEntry).

-   Play starts at @cpp "PlaylistIndex" @ce and advances after each round, wrapping around at the end.
-   @cpp "RandomizePlaylist" @ce shuffles the list on start and on every wrap.
-   A Cooperation entry with a story level continues through its episode's next levels with the same
    settings; the next entry applies only once the episode ends.
-   A server hosted from the menu uses the playlist only if the level selection is left at the playlist;
    otherwise @cpp "PlaylistIndex" @ce is @cpp -1 @ce and the chosen level plays until an admin changes it.

@subsection multiplayer-config-access Admins, whitelist and bans

Players are identified by a <b>Unique Player ID</b> generated by each installation. Players find their own
in <em>Options</em> → <em>User Profile</em> (the item copies it to the clipboard); admins can look up a
connected player with `/ip`.

Property | Effect
-------- | ------
@cpp "AdminUniquePlayerIDs" @ce | Players in this map may use admin commands. The value is reserved for a list of privileges, @cpp "*" @ce meaning all
@cpp "WhitelistedUniquePlayerIDs" @ce | If not empty, <b>only</b> these players can join. The value is a comment
@cpp "BannedUniquePlayerIDs" @ce | These players are rejected. The value is a comment, e.g. the reason
@cpp "BannedIPAddresses" @ce | The same for addresses, when the player's ID is not known

-   @cpp "ServerPassword" @ce is the simpler alternative to a whitelist: the server stays visible, but
    only players with the password can enter.
-   @cpp "RequiresDiscordAuth" @ce requires a running <b>Discord</b> client, limiting the server to Linux,
    macOS and Windows players.
-   @cpp "AllowCheats" @ce is off by default; when on, admins may cheat in any mode and other players only
    in Cooperation, always only on themselves.

@subsection multiplayer-config-anticheat Anti-cheat

Each client simulates its own player and the server checks that the reported movement is possible. The
check is lenient because latency, packet loss and frame rate make both sides drift: distance is measured
against <em>server</em> time with an allowance for bursts, either side's view of the player may explain a
move, and warps the client took slightly earlier are recognized. Only a clear deviation snaps the player
back.

@m_class{m-noindent}

Each violation adds to a suspicion score that fades within about a minute. Escalation:

1.  Every violation is logged with details and the current score
2.  A high score notifies the server console and all connected admins
3.  A sustained pattern (high score from several violations over at least ten seconds) kicks the player,
    only if @cpp "AntiCheatKick" @ce is enabled (the default). Admins are never kicked

@m_class{m-noindent}

Players with a slow connection weigh in less. A player kicked for cheating loses retained progression like
any other kicked player.

@subsection multiplayer-config-reload Applying changes

The file is read on start. `/refresh` re-reads it and re-applies the welcome message, the game mode and the
current playlist entry, so most changes need no restart. `/set` (see @ref multiplayer-commands) changes
properties only until the next `/refresh` or restart and never writes to the file.

@section multiplayer-visibility Public and private servers

Visibility is set by @cpp "IsPrivate" @ce (or the menu item the host used):

Behavior | Public server | Private server
-------- | ------------- | --------------
Announced on the local network | Yes, answers discovery on UDP port 7439 | No
Published to the online server list | Yes, to `https://de4th.dev/jazz2/servers` | No
Shown in <b>Join Server</b> | Yes, in both lists | No, enter the address
Requires a reachable address | Yes | Yes, for everyone given the address
Password, whitelist and bans | Apply | Apply

@m_class{m-block m-warning}

@par Private is not access control
Anyone who learns a private server's address can connect, so a friends-only server should be private
<b>and</b> password-protected.

@m_class{m-noindent}

A public server re-publishes itself every 5 minutes and delists on clean shutdown.

-   Behind NAT or in a container the self-detected address is useless --- set @cpp "ServerAddressOverride" @ce
    to the address (and port, if different) internet players should use. It takes one address or an array
    (e.g. IPv4 and IPv6, or a domain name plus an address), tried in order.
-   A domain name needs no separate entries per protocol: clients resolve it to all its addresses (up to four)
    and try each, so a name with both `A` and `AAAA` records works over either.
-   The port must be reachable: @cpp "ServerPort" @ce over <b>UDP</b>, and @cpp "WsPort" @ce over <b>TCP</b>
    if WebSocket is enabled.
-   `/endpoints` lists the known endpoints, including overrides.

@section multiplayer-joining Joining a server

Choose <b>Play Multiplayer</b> → <b>Join Server</b>. The list combines local network discovery and the
online list, showing name, player count, version and address; @cpp "ws" @ce marks WebSocket support,
@cpp "R" @ce reforged gameplay and @cpp "^" @ce a local network server. Joinable servers are listed first,
incompatible versions are highlighted. Private servers must be entered by address.

@m_class{m-noindent}

An endpoint is an IPv4 address, a bracketed IPv6 address or a domain name, optionally with a port
(default @cpp 7438 @ce), or a `ws://` / `wss://` URL for the WebSocket transport:

@code{.sh}
192.168.1.10          # Local network, default port
example.com:7438      # Domain name and an explicit port
[2001:db8::1]:7438    # IPv6 address
wss://example.com:443 # WebSocket transport (browsers always use this one)
@endcode

@m_class{m-noindent}

To connect directly on start (for a server browser or a shortcut), pass the endpoint and an optional
password:

@code{.sh}
./jazz2 /connect example.com:7438 secret123
@endcode

@m_class{m-noindent}

Joining goes through these steps:

1.  Protocol version check --- by default the client's version must match the server's exactly, patch
    included (see @ref NCINE_PROTOCOL_VERSION_MIN and @ref NCINE_PROTOCOL_VERSION_MAX). Converted levels
    are versioned per build too (see @ref Jazz2::Formats::LevelFile).
2.  Authentication against the whitelist, ban lists and password; a connection not authenticated within
    20 seconds is dropped.
3.  Missing assets (levels, tilesets, music) are downloaded if @cpp "AllowAssetStreaming" @ce is on;
    otherwise the player must install them manually.
4.  An in-game lobby offers character selection and shows the welcome message.

@m_class{m-noindent}

Reconnecting within @cpp "ReconnectWindowSecs" @ce after a dropped connection restores weapons, lives,
score, gems and championship points. A player removed by the server (kicked, banned, idle or cheating)
always starts over.

@section multiplayer-commands Server commands

Type commands into the in-game console (the <em>backquote</em> or <b>T</b> key by default) or a dedicated
server's standard input; anything else is chat. Available to every player:

Command | Description
------- | -----------
`/info` | Server name, current level and mode, player count, load and uptime
`/players` | Connected players with ping, points, kills, deaths and idle time
`/team <team>` | Requests `blue`, `red`, `green`, `yellow` or `auto`, if the current mode allows it
`/kill` | Kills your own player
`/vote <type>` | Starts a 60-second poll to `restart` the playlist, `skip` the level or `reset points`
`/yes` | Votes in the active poll

@m_class{m-noindent}

Admin commands require an entry in @cpp "AdminUniquePlayerIDs" @ce (a dedicated server's console is always
an admin):

Command | Description
------- | -----------
`/set mode <mode>` | Changes the game mode and restarts the round. Same values as @cpp "GameMode" @ce
`/set level <level>` | Loads another level and leaves the playlist
`/set name <name>` | Renames the server; an empty name stops publishing to the online list
`/set welcome <message>` | Changes the lobby welcome message, `\n` starts a new line
`/set kills`, `/set laps`, `/set treasure <n>` | Changes the win target of the current round
`/set teamcount <2-4>` | Changes the number of teams, re-splits them and restarts the round
`/set autobalance`, `/set teamselect`, `/set friendlyfire <on/off>` | Team rules of the current round
`/set cheats <on/off>` | Allows or forbids cheats
`/set anticheatkick <on/off>` | Allows or forbids anti-cheat kicks, see @ref multiplayer-config-anticheat
`/set spawning <on/off>` | Stops or resumes player spawning
`/next` | Ends the round and continues with the next one
`/playlist [<index>]` | Jumps to a playlist entry, or advances to the next one
`/skip` | Skips the remaining pre-game countdown
`/balance` | Rebalances the teams immediately
`/setteam <player#> <team>` | Moves a player to a team
`/reset points` | Resets everyone's championship points
`/alert <message>` | Shows a message over every player's HUD
`/kick <player>` | Disconnects a player, by name or by `#` and index
`/kill <player>` | Kills a player without counting a death
`/ip <player>` | Address and Unique Player ID of a player
`/endpoints` | Endpoints the server is reachable at, including @cpp "ServerAddressOverride" @ce
`/refresh` | Reloads the configuration file, see @ref multiplayer-config-reload
`/vote cancel` | Cancels the active poll
`/exit`, `/quit` | Shuts the dedicated server down (its own console only)

@section multiplayer-args Command-line arguments

Argument | Description
-------- | -----------
`/server [<config>]` | Runs as a dedicated server, see @ref multiplayer-hosting-dedicated. Also `--server`. Linux, macOS and debug Windows builds only; not needed in a `DEDICATED_SERVER` build, whose first argument is the configuration path
`/connect <endpoint> [<password>]` | Connects to a server on start instead of showing the main menu. Also `--connect` and `-c`. Not available in a `DEDICATED_SERVER` build
`/config <path>` | Path to the preferences file; relative server configuration paths are resolved against its directory
`/log` | Attaches a console to the process (<b>Windows</b> only, where non-server builds have none)
`/log:file[:<name>]` | Writes the trace log to a file in the preferences directory, @cpp "Jazz2.log" @ce by default

@m_class{m-noindent}

With the default name, the previous session's log is appended to @cpp "Jazz2.log.gz" @ce (one <b>gzip</b>
member per session, readable as one file, trimmed to the latest 4 MB or 32 sessions). An explicitly named
log file gets no archive.

*/

}
